  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Tuesday, a cold advection weather pattern \nwill persist bringing mostly cloudy, cool, and somewhat damp \nconditions, but little in the way of any measurable rainfall is \nexpected. \n\nFor Tuesday night through Wednesday, the primary concern is with \nthe strong shortwave trough that will amplify and cross the \nArkLaTex region, and eventually bring our area a 6 to 12 hr \nwindow of strong lift. Most guidance is now locked in on significant\nand beneficial rain to fall over central/eastern MS. For \nlocations east of the I-55 corridor, probabilistic rainfall \nforecasts now indicate low to medium chances (20 to 50%) for \ngreater than two inches. As of now, the long duration of rainfall \nsuggests we shouldn't see rain rates that lead to any flooding \nproblems, but dormant vegetative conditions could lead to some \nponding of water fairly quick. \n\nAnother forecast aspect is the much cooler temperatures that \naccompany the trough and polar high pressure surging southward \nthrough the mid MS Valley region. We should see temperatures dip \nto their lowest values later this week in the drier air following \nthe mid week system, with the potential for freezes both \nThanksgiving and Friday mornings. There has been some trend toward\nslower clearing, and this could lend to smaller diurnal \ntemperature ranges than currently forecast (i.e., cooler days and \nnot so cold nights) as we finish up the week.\n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, dry and milder conditions are \nexpected overall. There is a good bit of uncertainty regarding \nthe next rain chances as global models show a wide range of \nsolutions with how quickly the next Pacific stream trough will \napproach, especially by Sunday. /EC/",
